/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 547 0 R
/ActualText (Errors that occur because the compiler is unable to understand the written code are called Semantic Errors. )
/Alt ()
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
369 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 548 0 R
/ActualText (A semantic error will be generated if the code makes no sense to the compiler, even though it is syntactically correct. It is like using the wrong word in the wrong place in the English language. For example, adding a string to an integer will generate a semantic error.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
370 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 549 0 R
/ActualText (Semantic errors are different from syntax errors, as syntax errors signify that the structure of a program is incorrect without considering its meaning. On the other hand, semantic errors signify the incorrect implementation of a program by considering the meaning of the program.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
371 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 550 0 R
/ActualText (The most commonly occurring semantic errors are: use of un-initialized variables, type compatibility, and array index out of bounds.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
